::STAT_U02_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
What is the range of 5, 7, 12, 15?
{
=A. 10#Correct. The range is 15 minus 5, which equals 10.
~B. 5#5 is the minimum, not the range.
~C. 15#15 is the maximum, not the range.
~D. 20#This adds the maximum and minimum instead of subtracting.
}

::STAT_U02_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Two data sets have the same mean. Set A has smaller standard deviation than Set B. What does that mean?
{
~A. Set A has a larger mean#The means are stated to be the same.
=B. Set A is less spread out#Correct. A smaller standard deviation means values are typically closer to the mean.
~C. Set A has more categories#Standard deviation describes numerical spread, not category count.
~D. Set A has no variability#Smaller spread does not necessarily mean no spread.
}

::STAT_U02_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
What does interquartile range measure?
{
~A. The average value#Mean measures average, not IQR.
~B. The most common value#Mode measures most frequent value.
=C. Spread of the middle half of the data#Correct. IQR is Q3 minus Q1, describing the middle 50%.
~D. The total number of values#IQR measures spread, not count.
}

::STAT_U02_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Set A has values close together, and Set B has values far apart. Which set has greater variability?
{
~A. Set A#Close values show less spread.
~B. Both must have the same variability#The described spreads are different.
~C. Variability cannot be compared#Spread can be compared using range, IQR, MAD, or standard deviation.
=D. Set B#Correct. Values that are farther apart show greater spread.
}

::STAT_U02_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Why is variability important when comparing two data sets with the same median?
{
=A. It shows whether values are clustered or spread out#Correct. Center alone does not show consistency or spread.
~B. It changes the median automatically#Variability can differ even when medians are the same.
~C. It replaces the need for context#Context is still needed to interpret spread.
~D. It only applies to categorical data#Variability describes numerical data spread.
}

